{
  "0": "Training strategy: multiply the existing learning rate by 0.5, leaving optimizer type, batch size, dropout, weight decay and scheduler rules unchanged. Smaller updates may reduce the validation oscillation seen in Electronics while preserving gains in the other categories. Apply to all four datasets; retain seed 42, the 200-epoch cap, existing early stopping, architecture and loss. Select one checkpoint per category without averaging.",
  "1": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.8*h_last + 0.2*sum(w_t*h_t), where normalized weights w_t are proportional to 2^(-(L-1-t)/5) over valid recurrent states. For per-position supervision, pool only the causal prefix. This retains recent intent while smoothing noisy final states, potentially helping Electronics. Keep history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed across all four datasets; mirror the readout in the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Learning objective: replace the prediction loss with mean softplus(1 + s_negative - s_positive), using exactly the existing sampled negatives and valid target positions, averaging negatives within each target before averaging targets. The unit-margin pairwise objective may focus learning on positive-versus-negative separation relevant to HR@10. Apply to all four datasets, preserving sampling, features, GRU, readout, scoring, optimizer, scheduler and batch/epoch settings.",
  "3": "Network architecture: apply learned LayerNorm separately to each GRU gate's hidden-to-hidden affine contribution before combining it with the existing input contribution. Retain GRU reset/update equations, depth and hidden width. Stabilizing recurrent gate scales may improve optimization on heterogeneous histories without adding another recurrent layer. Preserve features, embeddings, history selection, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four datasets; update the predictor writer to load this single trained network per category."
}
